WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.900
When does the Master Theorem

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.940
fail? We will test its

3
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.920
required form, distinguish logarithmic and

4
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.320
polynomial gaps, and explain why

5
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.620
the regularity condition cannot simply

6
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:12.220
be ignored.

7
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.920
First check that the recurrence

8
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.580
really has a constant number

9
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.720
of equal-shrink subproblems. T of

10
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.140
n minus one plus n

11
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.160
does not have a fixed

12
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.680
shrink factor greater than one,

13
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.740
so this Master Theorem does

14
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.940
not directly apply.

15
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.360
Likewise, a call on n

16
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.260
over two and another on

17
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.200
n over three do not

18
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.300
fit the equal-size form. That

19
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.700
does not make the recurrence

20
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.860
unsolvable; it means you must

21
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.760
use another justified method.

22
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.680
Now consider three T of

23
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.260
n over three plus n

24
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.460
divided by log n, with

25
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.120
a suitable constant-size base. Its

26
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.920
form fits, and p equals

27
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.940
one, so the benchmark is

28
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:54.300
n.

29
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.180
The local work is smaller

30
00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.440
than n, because dividing by

31
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.400
log n makes its ratio

32
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.800
to n approach zero. But

33
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.220
case one needs more than

34
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.320
simply being smaller: it needs

35
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.080
a fixed polynomial gap.

36
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.040
Divide n over log n

37
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.240
by n to the one

38
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.100
minus epsilon. The ratio becomes

39
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.900
n to the epsilon over

40
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.020
log n, which grows without

41
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.640
limit for every fixed positive

42
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.180
epsilon, so the required case-one

43
00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.320
upper bound fails.

44
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.020
Case two does not apply

45
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.180
either, because n over log

46
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.000
n is not Theta of

47
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.300
n. The extended logarithmic version

48
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.260
supplied by this course requires

49
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.280
a nonnegative logarithmic exponent, whereas

50
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.160
this example would need minus

51
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:40.560
one.

52
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.700
Case three cannot help because

53
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.560
this local cost is smaller,

54
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.220
not polynomially larger, than the

55
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.960
benchmark. The example lies between

56
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.560
cases one and two of

57
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.580
the stated theorem, correcting the

58
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.280
case-number label on the slide.

59
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.440
A recursion-tree expansion offers a

60
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.100
way forward without forcing the

61
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.080
wrong theorem. At level i

62
00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.660
the combined work is n

63
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.180
divided by log of n

64
00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.580
over three to the i,

65
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.320
so you must sum those

66
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.000
varying level costs with a

67
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.280
proper base threshold.

68
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.380
Now look at the other

69
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.360
common omission, case-three regularity. A

70
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.640
large value of f at

71
00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.200
one scale does not guarantee

72
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.300
that the sum of the

73
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.660
child-scale costs contracts by a

74
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:25.540
constant factor.

75
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.000
For a clean counterexample, define

76
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.300
f of n as n

77
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:31.800
cubed at powers of two

78
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.740
and n otherwise, and consider

79
00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.380
T of n equals T

80
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.740
of n over three plus

81
00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:37.760
f of n on exact

82
00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.980
multiples. Although f is always

83
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.680
at least n, at n

84
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:42.940
equal to three times a

85
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.320
power of two the child

86
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.960
cost can be cubic while

87
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:47.300
the parent cost is only

88
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:47.720
linear.

89
00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.760
Consequently, no fixed contraction constant

90
00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.360
can establish regularity there, even

91
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.080
though f exceeds the constant

92
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.900
leaf benchmark by a polynomial

93
00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.080
factor. This is an explanatory

94
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.080
example of the condition, not

95
00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.420
a transcription of the slide

96
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.280
whose extracted formula is ambiguous.

97
00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.240
Things to take note of:

98
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.300
verify the recurrence shape and

99
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.040
every case condition before using

100
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.460
the conclusion. A logarithmic gap

101
00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.080
is not a polynomial gap,

102
00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.900
missing regularity matters, and iteration

103
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.320
or substitution remains available when

104
00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:21.680
this theorem has nothing to

105
00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.500
say, hmph.
